1
00:00:00,330 --> 00:00:01,970
This bee is giving directions.

2
00:00:02,209 --> 00:00:03,179
Her dance tells the

3
00:00:03,179 --> 00:00:04,495
colony where the food is.

4
00:00:05,412 --> 00:00:06,892
Inside the hive it's dark,

5
00:00:07,012 --> 00:00:08,712
and the comb is a vertical wall.

6
00:00:08,977 --> 00:00:10,097
She can't point at the

7
00:00:10,097 --> 00:00:11,167
flowers from in here.

8
00:00:11,422 --> 00:00:12,327
So she dances.

9
00:00:13,254 --> 00:00:13,911
On the comb,

10
00:00:14,194 --> 00:00:15,864
straight up means
toward the sun.

11
00:00:16,166 --> 00:00:18,404
Food forty-five degrees
right of the sun?

12
00:00:18,604 --> 00:00:20,405
Her waggle run leans forty-five

13
00:00:20,424 --> 00:00:21,894
degrees right of straight up.

14
00:00:22,233 --> 00:00:23,184
As the sun moves,

15
00:00:23,449 --> 00:00:25,007
she shifts her angle to match.

16
00:00:25,934 --> 00:00:27,784
She repeats the
loop, turning left,

17
00:00:27,974 --> 00:00:29,564
then right: a figure-eight.

18
00:00:29,841 --> 00:00:31,794
The waggle run
carries the distance.

19
00:00:32,145 --> 00:00:33,215
The farther the food,

20
00:00:33,264 --> 00:00:34,434
the longer she waggles.

21
00:00:35,338 --> 00:00:37,794
For very close food,
about ten metres,

22
00:00:37,918 --> 00:00:39,958
she runs in a small
circle instead.

23
00:00:40,448 --> 00:00:42,494
Past about forty
metres, the circle

24
00:00:42,545 --> 00:00:44,165
stretches into the waggle dance.

25
00:00:45,030 --> 00:00:47,878
Karl von Frisch, the
Austrian scientist

26
00:00:47,880 --> 00:00:50,740
who shared the 1973 Nobel Prize,

27
00:00:51,090 --> 00:00:52,810
was one of the
first to decode it.
